> and GruntHack. Maybe one day they will be as popular as
> Nethack and Slash'EM, but if they aren't, then thats
> just the way fate rolled them to be.


I've been arguing that the possibility for forks to happen is largely
irrelevant; the main project, the real deal, is where most coders'
contributions are aimed.  Few people are interested in starting
offshoot XYZhack projects, partly because managing a separate, new
game project is an onerous task in itself.  It's inefficient.  The
nethack gaming community doesn't seem to be interested in multifarious
parallel projects.  Most of the aficionado code is written for
nethack.  Most people who contribute want to contribute a little bit
of their time.   All this points to making nethack itself, the real
deal, more open and inclusive.

